\begin{abstract}
The UAS-CPE/BIO-G framework integrates real-time astrophysical telemetry, post-quantum homomorphic computation, relativistic magnetohydrodynamic (RMHD) and general relativistic (GR) tensor evolution, cryogenic quantum hardware acceleration, and synthetic octanucleotide genomic encoding into a unified end-to-end physical engine. Gaia DR3, JWST NIRCam/MIRI, and SDSS DR18 data streams are embedded into a perturbed Robertson--Walker metric and evolved through a 12-level AMR RMHD/GR solver that preserves stress-energy conservation, magnetic solenoidality, and Liouvillian phase-space invariants. All state transitions are attested via ML-KEM-1024 lattice cryptography and recursive Halo2/Plonky3 zero-knowledge proofs, enabling fully encrypted astrophysical simulation with verifiable physical fidelity.

Empirical laboratory validation demonstrates stable synthesis of 10{,}240-base-pair octanucleotide duplexes encoding astrophysical voxel tensors, with mass accuracy of 1.04~ppm, melting temperatures exceeding 94$^\circ$C, and canonical B-form helical geometry confirmed by circular dichroism spectroscopy. Cryogenic characterization of a 128-qubit transmon array at 14.8~mK yields mean coherence times of $T_1 = 88.4~\mu\mathrm{s}$ and $T_2^{\mathrm{echo}} = 94.1~\mu\mathrm{s}$, with simultaneous randomized benchmarking achieving 99.64\% two-qubit fidelity. Photonic direct-to-die interconnects sustain 800~Gbps bandwidth with post-FEC bit-error rates below $10^{-16}$, while CKKS homomorphic SIMD operations execute in 2.14~ms across 65{,}536 encrypted slots.

Together, these results establish UAS-CPE/BIO-G as the first unified architecture capable of securely evolving astrophysical state tensors, cryptographically verifying physical invariants, and encoding cosmological information into stable synthetic genomic substrates. The system represents a new class of multi-domain scientific engines bridging astrophysics, quantum computation, cryptography, and synthetic biology.
\end{abstract}